Analyze this lazy initialization pattern (double-check locking):

```java
class ExpensiveResource {
    private static ExpensiveResource instance = null;

    public static ExpensiveResource getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {  // Check 1: no lock
            synchronized(ExpensiveResource.class) {
                if (instance == null) {  // Check 2: with lock
                    instance = new ExpensiveResource();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Questions:
1. Why is double-check locking used here (what's the benefit)?
2. Is there a bug without volatile? Explain.
3. How does volatile fix the issue?

Answer format:
Benefit: [why two checks instead of one synchronized block]
Bug: [what can go wrong without volatile]
Volatile fix: [how volatile ensures thread safety]

